\details{
Descents give the step \code{du} from the Jacobian \code{J} and residual \code{fu}:
\code{NewtonDescent()} solves \verb{J du = -fu} (least squares when \code{J} is not
square), \code{SteepestDescent()} takes \verb{-J' fu}, \code{DampedNewtonDescent( initial_damping, damping_fn)} solves \verb{(J'J + lambda D) du = -J' fu},
\code{Dogleg()} the dogleg path inside a trust region,
\code{MoreTrustRegionDescent(scaling)} the exact trust region step of Moré
(MINPACK \code{lmpar}; \code{scaling = TrustRegionScaling$Jacobian} scales by the
column norms of \code{J}, \code{"Auto"} when they differ by more than 20), and
\code{GeodesicAcceleration(descent, finite_diff_step_geodesic, alpha)} adds the
second order geodesic correction to another descent.

\code{GeneralizedFirstOrderAlgorithm(descent, linesearch, trustregion)} runs a
descent with an optional line search (see \link{line_searches}) or trust
region scheme (\code{GenericTrustRegionScheme(method)} with \code{method} one of
\code{RadiusUpdateSchemes}). \code{TrustRegionDogleg()} is the trust region with the
dogleg subproblem and \code{TrustRegionRobust()} with Moré's subproblem;
\code{PseudoTransient(alpha_initial)} is the damped Newton method with the
switched evolution relaxation of Kelley and Keyes.

\code{QuasiNewtonAlgorithm(descent, linesearch, update_rule, initialization, max_resets)} updates an approximate Jacobian (\code{"good_broyden"},
\code{"bad_broyden"} or \code{"klement"}); \code{LimitedMemoryBroyden(threshold)} keeps
the last \code{threshold} Broyden updates of the inverse Jacobian.
\code{GeneralizedDFSane(linesearch, sigma_min, sigma_max)} and \code{DFSane()} are
the derivative free spectral methods of La Cruz, Martinez and Raydan.
\code{SimpleBroyden()}, \code{SimpleKlement()}, \code{SimpleDFSane()},
\code{SimpleGaussNewton()}, \code{SimpleLimitedMemoryBroyden()} and \code{SimpleHalley()}
(Halley's third order method, with the second derivatives by finite
differences) are the methods of SimpleNonlinearSolve.

All accept the \code{solve()} options \code{abstol}, \code{reltol}, \code{maxiters} and
\code{termination_condition} (see \link{termination_modes}).
}
